    solution_html: `
      <h4>1. Traditional Role vs. Advanced Role of the CFO</h4>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Traditional Role:</strong> Historically, the CFO was primarily a scorekeeper and custodian of corporate wealth. Responsibilities centered around maintaining financial health, enforcing statutory compliances, overseeing internal financial controls, book-keeping, and ensuring business ethics.</li>
        <li><strong>Advanced Leadership Role:</strong> In recent years, driven by globalization, technological disruptions, supply shocks, and multi-stakeholder expectations, the CFO has transitioned into a <strong>Strategic Business Partner and Value Architect</strong>. In addition to governance, CFOs now drive strategic decision-making, operational optimizations, and sustainable value creation over the long term.</li>
      </ul>

      <h4>2. Four Post-Pandemic Focus Areas</h4>
      <p>As recognized in the ICAI AFM curriculum, the CFO's leadership has advanced in the following four specific areas:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Risk Management:</strong> Directing and governing the comprehensive Enterprise Risk Management (ERM) system across the entire organization.</li>
        <li><strong>Supply Chain Management:</strong> Evaluating the financial viability, cash conversion cycles, and resilience of critical supply networks to support sustainable growth.</li>
        <li><strong>Mergers, Acquisitions, and Corporate Restructuring:</strong> Leading strategic target evaluation, synergy quantification, and financing structures to capture market share and prevent business collapse.</li>
        <li><strong>Environmental, Social, and Governance (ESG) Financing:</strong> Shifting from conventional funding to sustainable finance, green bonds, and ESG-linked debt instruments.</li>
      </ol>
    `
