    solution_html: `
      <h4>Six Parameters to Identify and Evaluate Currency Risk</h4>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Government Action:</strong> Major political and sovereign decisions exert immediate visual shocks on currency values. For example, the United Kingdom government's decision to exit the European Union (Brexit) sent the British Pound crashing to multi-decade historical lows.</li>
        <li><strong>Nominal Interest Rates (Interest Rate Parity - IRP):</strong> According to IRP, exchange rate differentials reflect interest rate spreads between nations. Sudden monetary policy rate hikes by foreign central banks (e.g. US Federal Reserve) trigger capital outflows from emerging markets, depreciating domestic currencies.</li>
        <li><strong>Inflation Rates (Purchasing Power Parity - PPP):</strong> High relative domestic inflation erodes purchasing power and price competitiveness. Over the long run, currencies of high-inflation nations depreciate relative to low-inflation currencies.</li>
        <li><strong>Natural Calamities:</strong> Widespread floods, earthquakes, or droughts severely disrupt export supply chains and agricultural output, widening current account deficits and exerting downward currency pressure.</li>
        <li><strong>War, Coup, and Rebellion:</strong> Geopolitical conflict and domestic instability trigger panic capital flight toward safe-haven currencies (USD, Swiss Franc, Gold), plunging the local currency into severe devaluation.</li>
        <li><strong>Change of Government:</strong> The arrival of a new political regime with protectionist policies, currency controls, or hostility toward foreign direct investment triggers capital repatriation and currency depreciation.</li>
      </ol>
    `
