    solution_html: `
      <div class="solution-container">
        <h4>Solution to Question 8: Expected NPV, Standard Deviation, and Coefficient of Variation for Project Ranking</h4>
        
        <p><strong>(a) Annual Expected Net Cash Flow (ENCF) and Standard Deviation ($sigma_t$):</strong></p>
        <p><em>For Project Surya:</em></p>
        <ul>
          <li>$\text{ENCF}_S = (0.30 \times 24,00,000) + (0.50 \times 18,00,000) + (0.20 \times 12,00,000)$<br>
            $= 7,20,000 + 9,00,000 + 2,40,000 = \mathbf{₹ 18,60,000}$</li>
          <li>$\text{Variance } (\sigma_t^2)_S = 0.30(24,00,000 - 18,60,000)^2 + 0.50(18,00,000 - 18,60,000)^2 + 0.20(12,00,000 - 18,60,000)^2$<br>
            $= 0.30(5,40,000)^2 + 0.50(-60,000)^2 + 0.20(-6,60,000)^2$<br>
            $= 0.30(2,91,60,00,000) + 0.50(3,60,00,000) + 0.20(4,35,60,00,000)$<br>
            $= 8,74,80,00,000 + 1,80,00,000 + 8,71,20,00,000 = 17,64,00,00,000 \text{ ₹}^2$</li>
          <li>$\sigma_S = \sqrt{17,64,00,00,000} = \mathbf{₹ 4,20,000}$</li>
        </ul>

        <p><em>For Project Vayu:</em></p>
        <ul>
          <li>$\text{ENCF}_V = (0.25 \times 30,00,000) + (0.50 \times 17,00,000) + (0.25 \times 6,00,000)$<br>
            $= 7,50,000 + 8,50,000 + 1,50,000 = \mathbf{₹ 17,50,000}$</li>
          <li>$\text{Variance } (\sigma_t^2)_V = 0.25(30,00,000 - 17,50,000)^2 + 0.50(17,00,000 - 17,50,000)^2 + 0.25(6,00,000 - 17,50,000)^2$<br>
            $= 0.25(12,50,000)^2 + 0.50(-50,000)^2 + 0.25(-11,50,000)^2$<br>
            $= 0.25(1,56,25,00,00,000) + 0.50(2,50,00,000) + 0.25(1,32,25,00,00,000)$<br>
            $= 39,06,25,00,000 + 1,25,00,000 + 33,06,25,00,000 = 72,25,00,00,000 \text{ ₹}^2$</li>
          <li>$\sigma_V = \sqrt{72,25,00,00,000} = \mathbf{₹ 8,50,000}$</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>(b) Computation of Expected Net Present Value (ENPV):</strong></p>
        <p>Using PVAF(8%, 3 years) = 2.5771:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>$\text{ENPV}_S = (18,60,000 \times 2.5771) - 40,00,000 = 47,93,406 - 40,00,000 = \mathbf{₹ 7,93,406}$</li>
          <li>$\text{ENPV}_V = (17,50,000 \times 2.5771) - 40,00,000 = 45,09,925 - 40,00,000 = \mathbf{₹ 5,09,925}$</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>(c) Standard Deviation of NPV ($sigma_{	ext{NPV}}$) for Independent Cash Flows:</strong></p>
        <p>When cash flows are mutually independent across periods, the variance of NPV is the sum of discounted periodic variances:</p>
        <p>$$\sigma_{\text{NPV}}^2 = \sum_{t=1}^3 \frac{\sigma_t^2}{(1 + R_f)^{2t}} = \sigma_t^2 \left[ \frac{1}{(1.08)^2} + \frac{1}{(1.08)^4} + \frac{1}{(1.08)^6} \right]$$</p>
        <p>Evaluating the discounting factors:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>$\frac{1}{(1.08)^2} = 0.85734$; $\frac{1}{(1.08)^4} = 0.73503$; $\frac{1}{(1.08)^6} = 0.63017$</li>
          <li>Sum of factors $= 0.85734 + 0.73503 + 0.63017 = 2.22254$</li>
        </ul>
        <p><em>For Project Surya:</em></p>
        <p>$$\sigma_{\text{NPV}, S}^2 = 17,64,00,00,000 \times 2.22254 = 39,20,56,05,600 \text{ ₹}^2$$</p>
        <p>$$\mathbf{\sigma_{\text{NPV}, S} = \sqrt{39,20,56,05,600} = ₹ 6,26,144}$$</p>

        <p><em>For Project Vayu:</em></p>
        <p>$$\sigma_{\text{NPV}, V}^2 = 72,25,00,00,000 \times 2.22254 = 1,60,57,85,15,000 \text{ ₹}^2$$</p>
        <p>$$\mathbf{\sigma_{\text{NPV}, V} = \sqrt{1,60,57,85,15,000} = ₹ 12,67,196}$$</p>

        <p><strong>(d) Computation of Coefficient of Variation ($CV$):</strong></p>
        <p>$$CV = \frac{\sigma_{\text{NPV}}}{\text{ENPV}}$$</p>
        <ul>
          <li>$$CV_S = \frac{6,26,144}{7,93,406} = \mathbf{0.7892}$$</li>
          <li>$$CV_V = \frac{12,67,196}{5,09,925} = \mathbf{2.4851}$$</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>(e) Comparative Evaluation and Decision:</strong></p>
        <table class="exam-table" border="1" cellpadding="6" cellspacing="0" style="width: 80%; margin: 15px 0;">
          <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <tr>
              <th style="text-align: left;">Evaluation Metric</th>
              <th style="text-align: right;">Project Surya</th>
              <th style="text-align: right;">Project Vayu</th>
              <th style="text-align: left;">Superior Alternative</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Expected Net Present Value (ENPV)</td><td style="text-align: right;">₹ 7,93,406</td><td style="text-align: right;">₹ 5,09,925</td><td>Project Surya (Higher by ₹2,83,481)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Standard Deviation ($sigma_{\text{NPV}}$)</td><td style="text-align: right;">₹ 6,26,144</td><td style="text-align: right;">₹ 12,67,196</td><td>Project Surya (Lower risk)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Coefficient of Variation ($CV$)</td><td style="text-align: right;">0.7892</td><td style="text-align: right;">2.4851</td><td>Project Surya (Much lower relative risk)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>(i) For a Risk-Neutral Investor:</strong> Selects purely on the basis of expected return, ignoring variance. <strong>Project Surya</strong> is selected because it offers a higher ENPV (₹7,93,406 vs. ₹5,09,925).</li>
          <li><strong>(ii) For a Risk-Averse Investor:</strong> Evaluates the trade-off between return and risk. <strong>Project Surya</strong> strictly dominates Project Vayu across every single dimension: it generates a higher expected NPV while simultaneously exhibiting less than half the total dispersion ($sigma$) and less than one-third the relative risk per rupee of return ($CV = 0.7892$ vs. $2.4851$). Hence, Project Surya is unambiguously chosen.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    `
