    solution_html: `
      <div class="solution-container">
        <h4>Solution to Question 21: Statutory Governance, Ethical Standards, and Liabilities of Registered Valuers</h4>
        
        <p><strong>(a) Statutory Role and Responsibilities under Section 247 & ICAI Valuation Standards:</strong></p>
        <p>Under Section 247(2) of the Companies Act, 2013 and the ICAI Valuation Standards, a Registered Valuer holds a fiduciary statutory responsibility to:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Impartial & True Valuation:</strong> Make an impartial, true, and fair valuation of any assets, shares, debentures, or net worth of the company.</li>
          <li><strong>Due Professional Care:</strong> Exercise reasonable care, technical competence, and due diligence in conducting valuation procedures.</li>
          <li><strong>Comprehensive Methodological Disclosure:</strong> State the valuation bases adopted (Asset Approach, Income Approach, Market Approach), explicitly justifying why specific methods were chosen or rejected.</li>
          <li><strong>Documentation of Limitations:</strong> Disclose in the Valuation Report all major assumptions, limitations, key inputs relied upon, and the extent of reliance placed on external expert opinions or management estimates.</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>(b) Essential Precautions Before Accepting a Valuation Assignment:</strong></p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Independence & Conflict of Interest Check:</strong> Verify that the valuer has no direct or indirect personal, financial, or professional interest in the client company or target asset (prior, present, or prospective for at least 3 years).</li>
          <li><strong>Technical Competence Evaluation:</strong> Ensure that the valuer possesses the specialized subject-matter expertise, tools, and staffing required to appraise complex assets (e.g., intangibles, fintech platforms, complex derivatives).</li>
          <li><strong>Formal Scope of Work / Engagement Letter:</strong> Execute a written engagement letter defining the exact valuation date, purpose, intended users, valuation base, and procedural scope to avoid subsequent liability scope creep.</li>
          <li><strong>Management Representation Letter (MRL):</strong> Procure an authorized written MRL confirming that all financial projections, historical statements, and operational data provided by management are complete, true, and free from material omissions.</li>
        </ol>

        <p><strong>(c) Professional Ethics & Avoidance of "Client-Pleasing" Valuations:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Objectivity over Advocacy:</strong> A valuer is an independent expert, not an advocate for the hiring client. The valuer must never accept an engagement where the conclusion or target valuation figure is predetermined by the client ("reverse engineering").</li>
          <li><strong>Rejection of Contingent Fees:</strong> Valuation fees linked to the outcome of a transaction (e.g., percentage of deal value or contingent upon deal closure) are strictly prohibited because they create an irreconcilable conflict of interest.</li>
          <li><strong>Confidentiality & Scepticism:</strong> Maintain strict professional secrecy over client data while maintaining healthy professional skepticism regarding aggressive hockey-stick financial projections supplied by promoters.</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>(d) Statutory Liabilities and Penalties under the Companies Act, 2013:</strong></p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>For Negligence / Contravention without Fraudulent Intent [Section 247(3)]:</strong>
            <ul>
              <li>Punishable with a fine which shall not be less than <strong>₹25,000</strong> but which may extend to <strong>₹1,00,000</strong>.</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li><strong>For Fraudulent Valuation with Intent to Defraud [Section 247(4)]:</strong>
            <p>If the valuer is convicted of acting with intent to defraud the company, its shareholders, or creditors:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Imprisonment:</strong> Punishable with imprisonment for a term which may extend to <strong>one year</strong>; AND</li>
              <li><strong>Enhanced Fine:</strong> Fine which shall not be less than <strong>₹1,00,000</strong> but which may extend to <strong>₹5,00,000</strong>; AND</li>
              <li><strong>Disgorgement / Refund of Remuneration:</strong> The valuer shall be liable to <strong>refund the entire remuneration</strong> received; AND</li>
              <li><strong>Damages for Losses:</strong> The valuer shall pay full <strong>damages to the company, creditors, or any other person</strong> for any loss sustained by them in consequence of the incorrect or misleading statements made in his report.</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    `
