    solution_html: `
      <div class="solution-container">
        <h4>Solution to Question 2: Minimum Variance Portfolio (MVP) and Riskless Hedging</h4>
        
        <p><strong>(a) Optimal Investment Proportions for Minimum Variance Portfolio (MVP):</strong></p>
        <p>First, calculate Covariance ($	ext{Cov}_{AB}$):</p>
        <p>$$	ext{Cov}_{AB} = ho_{AB} 	imes sigma_A 	imes sigma_B = -0.20 	imes 15 	imes 25 = mathbf{-75}$$</p>
        <p>The weight of Stock A in the Minimum Variance Portfolio is given by the formula:</p>
        <p>$$w_A^* = rac{sigma_B^2 - 	ext{Cov}_{AB}}{sigma_A^2 + sigma_B^2 - 2 	ext{Cov}_{AB}}$$</p>
        <p>Substitute the values:</p>
        <p>$$w_A^* = rac{625 - (-75)}{225 + 625 - 2(-75)} = rac{625 + 75}{850 + 150} = rac{700}{1,000} = mathbf{0.70 	ext{ (or 70.00%)}}$$</p>
        <p>Since $w_A^* + w_B^* = 1$:</p>
        <p>$$w_B^* = 1 - 0.70 = mathbf{0.30 	ext{ (or 30.00%)}}$$</p>

        <p><strong>(b) Expected Return and Standard Deviation of MVP:</strong></p>
        <p>1. Expected Return:</p>
        <p>$$E(R_{MVP}) = w_A^* E(R_A) + w_B^* E(R_B) = (0.70 	imes 12%) + (0.30 	imes 18%) = 8.40% + 5.40% = mathbf{13.80%}$$</p>
        <p>2. Portfolio Variance:</p>
        <p>$$sigma_{MVP}^2 = (w_A^*)^2 sigma_A^2 + (w_B^*)^2 sigma_B^2 + 2 w_A^* w_B^* 	ext{Cov}_{AB}$$</p>
        <p>$$sigma_{MVP}^2 = (0.70)^2 (225) + (0.30)^2 (625) + 2(0.70)(0.30)(-75)$$</p>
        <p>$$sigma_{MVP}^2 = (0.49 	imes 225) + (0.09 	imes 625) + (0.42 	imes -75)$$</p>
        <p>$$sigma_{MVP}^2 = 110.25 + 56.25 - 31.50 = mathbf{135.00}$$</p>
        <p>Portfolio Standard Deviation:</p>
        <p>$$sigma_{MVP} = sqrt{135.00} = mathbf{11.62%}$$</p>

        <p><strong>(c) Economic Significance of $sigma_{MVP} < sigma_A$:</strong></p>
        <p>The least risky individual asset is Stock A with $sigma_A = 15.00%$. However, combining Stock A with an even riskier asset (Stock B with $sigma_B = 25.00%$) creates a portfolio with $sigma_{MVP} = 11.62%$.</p>
        <p>This demonstrates the fundamental power of portfolio diversification: when assets have low or negative correlation ($ho < rac{sigma_A}{sigma_B} = rac{15}{25} = 0.60$), the diversification effect eliminates sufficient covariance risk such that the total portfolio risk falls strictly below the risk of the least risky individual asset.</p>

        <p><strong>(d) Case where $ho_{AB} = -1.0$ (Perfect Negative Correlation):</strong></p>
        <p>When correlation is perfectly negative, risk can be completely eliminated ($sigma_p = 0$).</p>
        <p>Formula for zero-risk weights:</p>
        <p>$$w_A = rac{sigma_B}{sigma_A + sigma_B} = rac{25}{15 + 25} = rac{25}{40} = mathbf{0.625 	ext{ (or 62.50%)}}$$</p>
        <p>$$w_B = 1 - 0.625 = mathbf{0.375 	ext{ (or 37.50%)}}$$</p>
        <p>Verification of zero risk:</p>
        <p>$$sigma_p = |w_A sigma_A - w_B sigma_B| = |(0.625 	imes 15) - (0.375 	imes 25)| = |9.375 - 9.375| = mathbf{0.00%}$$</p>
        <p>Expected Return of this Zero-Risk Portfolio:</p>
        <p>$$E(R_p) = (0.625 	imes 12%) + (0.375 	imes 18%) = 7.50% + 6.75% = mathbf{14.25%}$$</p>
        <div class="summary-box" style="background-color: #e8f5e9; padding: 12px; border-left: 4px solid #2e7d32; margin-top: 15px;">
          <strong>Summary:</strong> Under perfect negative correlation, an investor achieves a guaranteed return of 14.25% with zero risk. In efficient financial markets, this return must equal the prevailing risk-free rate ($R_f$) to prevent pure arbitrage.
        </div>
      </div>
    `
