    solution_html: `
      <div class="solution-container">
        <h4>Solution to Question 11: CML vs SML Valuation and Systematic Risk Decomposition</h4>
        
        <p><strong>(a) Capital Market Line (CML) Equation and Evaluation of Portfolio P:</strong></p>
        <p>The CML relates expected return to total risk ($sigma$) for efficient portfolios:</p>
        <p>$$E(R_p) = R_f + left(rac{E(R_m) - R_f}{sigma_m}ight) sigma_p$$</p>
        <p>$$	ext{Slope of CML (Sharpe Ratio of Market)} = rac{14.00% - 6.00%}{15.00%} = rac{8.00%}{15.00%} = mathbf{0.5333}$$</p>
        <p>CML Equation: $$E(R_p) = 6.00% + 0.5333 	imes sigma_p$$</p>
        <p>For Portfolio P ($sigma_P = 22.50%$):</p>
        <p>$$E(R_P)_{	ext{CML}} = 6.00% + (0.5333 	imes 22.50%) = 6.00% + 12.00% = mathbf{18.00%}$$</p>
        <p>Abnormal Performance:</p>
        <p>$$	ext{Abnormal Return} = 	ext{Actual Return} - 	ext{CML Return} = 18.50% - 18.00% = mathbf{+0.50%}$$</p>
        <p>Portfolio P outperforms the CML benchmark by 50 basis points.</p>

        <p><strong>(b) Why CML Cannot Price Individual Stock S:</strong></p>
        <p>The Capital Market Line measures risk strictly by <strong>total standard deviation ($sigma$)</strong>. It applies exclusively to <em>efficient, fully-diversified portfolios</em> that lie on the efficient frontier and contain zero unsystematic risk.</p>
        <p>Individual stocks like Stock S contain substantial unsystematic (firm-specific) risk. In an efficient capital market, investors can diversify away unsystematic risk at zero cost; hence, the market provides no risk premium for bearing unsystematic risk. Evaluating Stock S on the CML would incorrectly demand compensation for diversifiable risk. Individual securities must be evaluated using the <strong>Security Market Line (SML)</strong>, where risk is measured by systematic beta ($eta$).</p>

        <p><strong>(c) Beta, SML Required Return, and Alpha of Stock S:</strong></p>
        <p>1. Calculation of Beta ($eta_S$):</p>
        <p>$$eta_S = ho_{Sm} 	imes rac{sigma_S}{sigma_m} = 0.60 	imes rac{25.00%}{15.00%} = 0.60 	imes 1.6667 = mathbf{1.00}$$</p>
        <p>2. SML / CAPM Required Return:</p>
        <p>$$E(R_S)_{	ext{SML}} = R_f + eta_S [E(R_m) - R_f] = 6.00% + 1.00 	imes (14.00% - 6.00%) = mathbf{14.00%}$$</p>
        <p>3. Jensen's Alpha:</p>
        <p>$$alpha_S = 	ext{Actual Return} - 	ext{Required Return} = 16.00% - 14.00% = mathbf{+2.00%}$$</p>
        <p>Stock S offers a positive alpha of +2.00% and is undervalued.</p>

        <p><strong>(d) Decomposition of Total Risk (Variance) of Stock S:</strong></p>
        <p>Total Variance: $$sigma_S^2 = (25.00)^2 = mathbf{625.00}$$</p>
        <p>Systematic Variance: $$	ext{Systematic Risk} = eta_S^2 	imes sigma_m^2 = (1.00)^2 	imes 225.00 = mathbf{225.00}$$</p>
        <p>Unsystematic (Residual) Variance: $$sigma_{eS}^2 = sigma_S^2 - 	ext{Systematic Risk} = 625.00 - 225.00 = mathbf{400.00}$$</p>
        <p>Unsystematic Standard Deviation: $$sigma_{eS} = sqrt{400.00} = mathbf{20.00%}$$</p>
        <div class="summary-box" style="background-color: #f9f9f9; padding: 12px; border-left: 4px solid #2196F3; margin-top: 15px;">
          <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Only 36% ($rac{225}{625}$) of Stock S's total variance represents market-priced systematic risk; the remaining 64% ($rac{400}{625}$) is unrewarded firm-specific risk, confirming why SML is the only theoretically sound pricing model.
        </div>
      </div>
    `
