    solution_html: `
      <div class="solution-container">
        <h4>Solution to Question 15: Construction of Pure Arbitrage Portfolio</h4>
        
        <p><strong>(a) Synthetic Replication of Portfolio 3 using Portfolios 1 and 2:</strong></p>
        <p>To replicate Portfolio 3 ($eta_3 = 0.90$), the synthetic portfolio must satisfy two conditions:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Weight constraint: $$w_1 + w_2 = 1.00 implies w_2 = 1.00 - w_1$$</li>
          <li>Beta matching constraint: $$w_1 eta_1 + w_2 eta_2 = eta_3 implies 1.20 w_1 + 0.70 (1.00 - w_1) = 0.90$$</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Solve for $w_1$:</p>
        <p>$$1.20 w_1 + 0.70 - 0.70 w_1 = 0.90$$</p>
        <p>$$0.50 w_1 = 0.90 - 0.70 = 0.20 implies w_1 = rac{0.20}{0.50} = mathbf{0.40 	ext{ (or 40.00%)}}$$</p>
        <p>$$w_2 = 1.00 - 0.40 = mathbf{0.60 	ext{ (or 60.00%)}}$$</p>
        <p>Expected return of the synthetic twin portfolio:</p>
        <p>$$E(R_{	ext{synth}}) = w_1 E(R_1) + w_2 E(R_2) = (0.40 	imes 16.00%) + (0.60 	imes 11.00%)$$</p>
        <p>$$E(R_{	ext{synth}}) = 6.40% + 6.60% = mathbf{13.00%}$$</p>

        <p><strong>(b) Verification of Arbitrage Opportunity:</strong></p>
        <p>Both the synthetic portfolio and Portfolio 3 possess identical systematic factor exposure ($eta = 0.90$).</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Actual Portfolio 3 offers an expected return of: <strong>15.00%</strong></li>
          <li>Synthetic Portfolio offers an expected return of: <strong>13.00%</strong></li>
        </ul>
        <p>Under the Law of One Price, two portfolios with identical factor risks must offer identical returns. Since Portfolio 3 delivers 2.00% higher return ($15.00% - 13.00% = +2.00%$) for the exact same risk, <strong>Portfolio 3 is undervalued (underpriced)</strong> relative to Portfolios 1 and 2. A pure arbitrage opportunity exists!</p>

        <p><strong>(c) Construction of Zero-Investment Arbitrage Portfolio (₹100 Lakhs Base):</strong></p>
        <p>To exploit this mispricing, the trader should <strong>Buy (Long)</strong> the undervalued Portfolio 3 and <strong>Sell (Short)</strong> the synthetic overvalued equivalent (Portfolios 1 and 2):</p>
        <table class="exam-table" border="1" cellpadding="6" cellspacing="0" style="width: 85%; margin: 15px 0;">
          <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <tr>
              <th style="text-align: left;">Position</th>
              <th style="text-align: center;">Portfolio</th>
              <th style="text-align: center;">Weight ($w$)</th>
              <th style="text-align: right;">Cash Flow / Investment (₹ Lakhs)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr style="background-color: #e8f5e9;">
              <td style="font-weight: bold;">Long Position</td>
              <td style="text-align: center;">Portfolio 3</td>
              <td style="text-align: center;">+1.00</td>
              <td style="text-align: right; color: green; font-weight: bold;">-₹ 100.00 Lakhs (Outflow)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="background-color: #fff3e0;">
              <td style="font-weight: bold;">Short Position</td>
              <td style="text-align: center;">Portfolio 1</td>
              <td style="text-align: center;">-0.40</td>
              <td style="text-align: right; color: blue; font-weight: bold;">+₹ 40.00 Lakhs (Inflow)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="background-color: #fff3e0;">
              <td style="font-weight: bold;">Short Position</td>
              <td style="text-align: center;">Portfolio 2</td>
              <td style="text-align: center;">-0.60</td>
              <td style="text-align: right; color: blue; font-weight: bold;">+₹ 60.00 Lakhs (Inflow)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="font-weight: bold; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
              <td colspan="2" style="text-align: center;">Net Arbitrage Portfolio</td>
              <td style="text-align: center;">0.00</td>
              <td style="text-align: right;">₹ 0.00 Lakhs (Zero Net Capital)</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p><strong>(d) Guaranteed Arbitrage Profit:</strong></p>
        <p>Percentage Arbitrage Return:</p>
        <p>$$R_{	ext{arb}} = w_3 E(R_3) + w_1 E(R_1) + w_2 E(R_2)$$</p>
        <p>$$R_{	ext{arb}} = (+1.00 	imes 15.00%) + (-0.40 	imes 16.00%) + (-0.60 	imes 11.00%)$$</p>
        <p>$$R_{	ext{arb}} = 15.00% - 6.40% - 6.60% = mathbf{+2.00%}$$</p>
        <p>Rupee Arbitrage Profit:</p>
        <p>$$	ext{Rupee Profit} = ₹ 100.00 	ext{ Lakhs} 	imes 2.00% = mathbf{₹ 2.00 	ext{ Lakhs (₹ 2,00,000)}}$$</p>

        <p><strong>(e) Mathematical Proof of Arbitrage Conditions:</strong></p>
        <ol type="i">
          <li><strong>Zero Net Wealth Committed:</strong>
            $$sum w_i = w_3 + w_1 + w_2 = (+1.00) + (-0.40) + (-0.60) = mathbf{0.00}$$
            The ₹100 Lakhs required to buy Portfolio 3 is completely funded by the short sale proceeds of Portfolio 1 (₹40 Lakhs) and Portfolio 2 (₹60 Lakhs).</li>
          <li><strong>Zero Systematic Factor Risk ($eta_{	ext{arb}} = 0$):</strong>
            $$eta_{	ext{arb}} = w_3 eta_3 + w_1 eta_1 + w_2 eta_2 = (1.00 	imes 0.90) + (-0.40 	imes 1.20) + (-0.60 	imes 0.70)$$
            $$eta_{	ext{arb}} = 0.90 - 0.48 - 0.42 = mathbf{0.00}$$
        </li>
        </ol>
        <div class="summary-box" style="background-color: #f9f9f9; padding: 12px; border-left: 4px solid #4CAF50; margin-top: 15px;">
          <strong>Conclusion:</strong> The trading desk captures a guaranteed, riskless return of ₹2,00,000 with zero net capital and zero factor risk. In equilibrium, such trades drive up the price of Portfolio 3 until its return falls to 13.00%, eliminating the arbitrage.
        </div>
      </div>
    `
