    solution_html: `
      <div class="solution-container">
        <h4>Solution to Question 12: Prepayment Benchmark Modeling (CPR / SMM) and MBS Negative Convexity</h4>
        
        <p><strong>(a) Definition of 100% PSA Prepayment Benchmark:</strong></p>
        <p>The Public Securities Association (PSA) benchmark models historical prepayment behavior for a 30-year residential mortgage pool as follows:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Ramp-Up Period (Months $t = 1$ to $30$):</strong> The annualized CPR begins at 0.20% in Month 1 and increases linearly by 0.20% each month:
            $$	ext{CPR}_t = 0.20% 	imes t quad (	ext{for } 1 le t le 30)$$
            At Month 30, the prepayment rate reaches: $0.20% 	imes 30 = mathbf{6.00% 	ext{ p.a.}}$</li>
          <li><strong>Plateau Period (Months $t = 31$ to $360$):</strong> For the remainder of the pool's life, the prepayment rate remains constant at <strong>6.00% p.a.</strong></li>
        </ul>
        <p>Prepayment speeds are expressed as multiples of the standard benchmark: e.g., 200% PSA implies prepayments occur at twice the standard rate (starting at 0.40% and plateauing at 12.00%).</p>

        <p><strong>(b) Calculation of Single Monthly Mortality (SMM) for CPR = 8.00%:</strong></p>
        <p>The Conditional Prepayment Rate (CPR) is the annualized percentage of outstanding principal prepaid. The Single Monthly Mortality (SMM) is the percentage prepaid in a single month.</p>
        <p>Formula connecting CPR and SMM:</p>
        <p>$$	ext{SMM} = 1 - (1 - 	ext{CPR})^{1/12}$$</p>
        <p>Given $	ext{CPR} = 0.08$:</p>
        <p>$$	ext{SMM} = 1 - (1 - 0.08)^{1/12} = 1 - (0.92)^{0.083333} = 1 - 0.993077 = mathbf{0.006923 	ext{ (or 0.6923% per month)}}$$</p>
        <p><em>Financial Meaning:</em> In any given month, approximately <strong>0.6923%</strong> of the loan pool principal balance remaining after scheduled contractual amortization is prepaid ahead of schedule by borrowers.</p>

        <p><strong>(c) Negative Convexity in Mortgage-Backed Securities:</strong></p>
        <p>Conventional option-free Treasury bonds possess <strong>positive convexity</strong>: as yields fall, price increases accelerate at an increasing rate, while as yields rise, price declines decelerate.</p>
        <p>MBS contain an embedded <strong>prepayment option</strong> held by the borrower (economically equivalent to the investor selling a call option on bond prices to the borrower):</p>
        <ul>
          <li>When interest rates fall significantly below the contractual loan rate, homeowners exercise their embedded refinancing option. Mortgages are prepaid at par.</li>
          <li>The price of the MBS becomes capped near par value; instead of accelerating upward, the price-yield curve flattens and curves downward to the left.</li>
          <li>This phenomenon is termed <strong>Negative Convexity</strong>: capital appreciation is strictly curtailed in bull markets (due to prepayments), while full downside capital losses are suffered in bear markets (as prepayments dry up and maturities extend).</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>(d) Hedging Mortgage Servicing Rights (MSR) with Stripped Securities:</strong></p>
        <p><strong>Problem:</strong> Mortgage Servicing Rights (MSR) generate a fixed percentage fee on outstanding loan balances. When rates fall, prepayments accelerate, principal disappears, and the value of MSR collapses (negative duration).</p>
        <p><strong>Hedging Solution:</strong> The fund manager should <strong>BUY Principal-Only (PO) Strips</strong>:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>PO strips have massive positive price exposure to accelerating prepayments (super-long positive duration).</li>
          <li>When interest rates plunge and refinancing surges, the heavy losses suffered on the MSR portfolio are directly offset and compensated by the exponential surge in the market value of the PO strips.</li>
          <li>Alternatively, if the manager had a traditional long Treasury bond portfolio and wished to hedge against rising interest rates, they would buy <strong>Interest-Only (IO) strips</strong>, because IO strips appreciate when rising rates extinguish prepayments (acting as a negative-duration asset).</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    `
