    placement: 1,
    marks: 4,
    question_html: `<p><strong>Question 19 [Theory &mdash; 4 Marks]</strong></p>
<p>The regulatory and statutory architecture governing Indian startups encompasses venture capital fund structures, tax incentives, and governance continuity. Answer the following questions:</p>
<ol type="a">
  <li>Explain the regulatory framework of <strong>Alternative Investment Funds (AIFs)</strong> under the SEBI (AIF) Regulations, 2012, highlighting the specific defining characteristics of <strong>Category I AIF &mdash; Venture Capital Funds (VCF) and Angel Funds</strong>.</li>
  <li>Enumerate the statutory eligibility criteria mandated by the Department for Promotion of Industry and Internal Trade (DPIIT) for an entity to be recognized as a <strong>"Startup"</strong> in India.</li>
  <li>Explain the tax incentives available to DPIIT-recognized startups under <strong>Section 80-IAC</strong> of the Income Tax Act, 1961, and discuss the landmark significance of the abolition of the <strong>"Angel Tax" (Section 56(2)(viib))</strong> in the Union Budget 2024.</li>
  <li>Outline the four-step framework of <strong>Succession Planning in Business</strong> and explain why mitigating "Founder Key-Man Risk" is crucial for startup survival.</li>
</ol>`,
    solution_html: `<p><strong>Solution 19: Regulatory Framework: SEBI AIFs, DPIIT Incentives &amp; Succession Planning</strong></p>

<p><strong>(a) SEBI Alternative Investment Funds (AIF) Framework &amp; Category I AIF:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>Under the <strong>SEBI (Alternative Investment Funds) Regulations, 2012</strong>, an AIF is a privately pooled investment vehicle established in India that collects funds from sophisticated Indian or foreign investors for investing in accordance with a defined investment policy.</li>
  <li><strong>Category I AIF:</strong> Funds that invest in early-stage ventures, social ventures, SMEs, and infrastructure that the government considers economically and socially desirable:
    <ol>
      <li><em>Venture Capital Funds (VCF):</em> Invest primarily in unlisted equity or equity-linked instruments of startups and early-stage companies.</li>
      <li><em>Angel Funds:</em> A sub-category of VCF that pools capital from Angel Investors (individual net worth $ge ₹10$ Crore or corporate net worth $ge ₹10$ Crore), with minimum investment tickets of ₹25 Lakh and a maximum investment cap of ₹10 Crore per startup.</li>
    </ol>
  </li>
  <li><em>Pass-Through Status:</em> Category I AIFs enjoy statutory tax pass-through status under Section 115UB of the Income Tax Act (income is taxed in the hands of the investor, not at the fund level).</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>(b) DPIIT Startup Recognition Eligibility Criteria:</strong></p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Period of Existence:</strong> The entity must not have completed <strong>10 years</strong> from the date of its incorporation/registration.</li>
  <li><strong>Entity Structure:</strong> Must be incorporated as a Private Limited Company, Registered Partnership Firm, or Limited Liability Partnership (LLP).</li>
  <li><strong>Turnover Threshold:</strong> Annual turnover must not have exceeded <strong>₹100 Crore</strong> in any preceding financial year since incorporation.</li>
  <li><strong>Innovation &amp; Scalability:</strong> The entity must be working towards innovation, development, or commercial improvement of products, processes, or services, or possess a scalable business model with high potential for employment generation and wealth creation.</li>
  <li><strong>Original Entity:</strong> Must not have been formed by splitting up or reconstructing a business already in existence.</li>
</ol>

<p><strong>(c) Section 80-IAC Tax Holiday &amp; Angel Tax Abolition:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Section 80-IAC Deduction:</strong> Eligible DPIIT-recognized startups incorporated between April 1, 2016 and March 31, 2025 can claim a <strong>100% tax holiday on operating profits for 3 consecutive financial years</strong> out of their first 10 years of operations.</li>
  <li><strong>Abolition of "Angel Tax" (Section 56(2)(viib)):</strong> Historically, Section 56(2)(viib) taxed the excess share premium received by an unlisted company over its Fair Market Value (FMV) as "Income from Other Sources". This created severe tax harassment for loss-making startups raising capital at premium valuations. The <strong>Union Budget 2024 completely abolished the Angel Tax for all classes of investors</strong>, removing a major capital formation barrier and liberating the Indian startup ecosystem.</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>(d) Succession Planning Framework &amp; Founder Key-Man Risk:</strong></p>
<p><em>The Four-Step Succession Framework:</em></p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Identify Critical Roles:</strong> Map indispensable leadership positions (CEO, CTO, Lead Architect).</li>
  <li><strong>Profile Success Competencies:</strong> Define the technical, managerial, and visionary capabilities required for future leadership.</li>
  <li><strong>Assess Talent &amp; Bench Strength:</strong> Identify internal high-potential successors and assess readiness gaps.</li>
  <li><strong>Develop Transition Pipelines:</strong> Implement targeted executive mentoring, equity vesting alignments, and cross-functional rotations.</li>
</ol>
<p><em>Founder Key-Man Risk:</em> Early-stage startups depend overwhelmingly on the specialized vision, coding capability, and investor relationships of 1 or 2 founders. Sudden death, incapacitation, or departure of a founder can instantly collapse customer trust and halt fundraising. Succession planning, coupled with <strong>Key-Man Insurance</strong>, mitigates this existential vulnerability.</p>`
